[- Reference: Streaming. -]
[page id='ref-streaming'
  [meta
    title='Streaming'
    layout='reference'
    nav-order='445'
    audience='user'
    summary='Event-driven parse and emit. Bounded RSS regardless of input size.'
  ]

  [section id='intro' title='When to Stream'
    [prose Streaming parse and emit move bytes through the runtime without ever holding the whole tree in memory. Use streaming when the input is large enough that resident memory matters — log files, telemetry dumps, large XML / JSON / CXDB sources. The tree API materialises everything; streaming materialises nothing.]
  ]

  [section id='events' title='Event Shape'
    [prose A streaming parse fires four event kinds: element_start (name plus attributes), attribute (after element_start, before any body), body chunk (text or scalar value), and element_end. The same event shape works on every input format — XML and JSON sources produce the same events.]
  ]

  [section id='read' title='Streaming Parse'
    [code lang='python' src='''import cx
with open('events.cx', 'rb') as f:
    for event in cx.stream(f):
        if event.kind == 'element_start' and event.name == 'error':
            print(event.attrs)''']
  ]

  [section id='write' title='Streaming Emit'
    [code lang='python' src='''with cx.stream_writer(open('out.cx', 'wb')) as w:
    for record in fetch_records():
        w.write(record)''']
    [prose RSS stays bounded regardless of the output size. Tier-1 bindings (V, Python, Go) ship the streaming writer at v0.7.0; Tier-2/3 follow in the v0.7.x window.]
  ]

  [section id='throughput' title='Throughput'
    [prose The streaming evaluator runs at 340 MB/s on the comparable bench corpus (M2 Pro, libcx -prod) — about 17 percent under the comparable JSON benchmark on the same workload. The bench harness lives in vcx/tests/runners/streaming_bench.v; run make bench to refresh.]
  ]

  [section id='backpressure' title='Backpressure'
    [prose The streaming sink uses a flush_after_bytes threshold to bound buffer residency. Default 64 KiB; lower for memory-constrained environments, higher to amortise callback overhead on small-emit workloads.]
  ]

